 "finish_summary": "Explored several alternative implementations of the 32-bit registered adder (a Kogge-Stone parallel-prefix adder, and a restructured single-level enable/reset mux version) and compared them against the original specification RTL using estimate_ppa and place_and_route. All alternatives either matched or slightly underperformed the original coding style: the Kogge-Stone adder gave essentially the same critical-path delay (ABC's own logic optimization already produces a near-optimal, low-depth carry network regardless of how the RTL expresses the addition) while using more area, and the restructured enable/reset mux (which reduces the adder's output to a single mux stage) actually resulted in a slightly worse routed score (74.064 vs 74.126) despite a smaller area estimate, because the achieved WNS dropped enough to offset the area savings (WNS is weighted 10x versus area's 0.001x in the reward). A direct place-and-route run of the original specification RTL confirmed the baseline result (area 746.13 um^2, WNS 7.48722 ns, score 74.12607), matching the reference measurement and outperforming every variant tried. Given the reward is dominated by WNS and the synthesis backend (Yosys/ABC to Nangate45) already restructures the addition/mux/reset logic into a near-optimal low-depth network irrespective of RTL coding style, no further improvement was found within the explored design space. I am finishing with the verified baseline result as the best-scoring, fully equivalent implementation (score 74.12607).",
